# DTF tier manifest: curation-ascii-lookalikes-upgrade  (artifact-keeper#5840, 1.13.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem + the mock npm upstream (upstreams.mock-npm).
#
# 1.13.0 adds the `popularity` rule setting `ascii_lookalikes` ("block" |
# "flag"). It decides whether a download is refused when the name is all
# ASCII and its confusable skeleton matches a popular name (`paypa1` for
# `paypal`). New rules default to "flag". Through 1.12 a
# `block_typosquat: true` rule refused those names, and an upgrade must not
# weaken an existing install, so migration 421 stamps "block" on every
# existing `block_typosquat` rule.
#
# PHASE 1 (UPGRADE_OLD_IMAGE, 1.12.4): an npm Remote with curation on and a
#   popularity rule {download_name_checks, block_typosquat, popular
#   ["paypal"]}, written the 1.12 way (no ascii_lookalikes). It refuses
#   `paypa1` there.
# PHASE 2 (the candidate, same volume and database):
#   FIX #5840  the existing rule carries ascii_lookalikes "block" and still
#              refuses `paypa1` with 403 curation_blocked.
#   FIX #5840  a rule created after the upgrade the same way (no
#              ascii_lookalikes) on a second Remote does NOT refuse
#              `paypa1` (a flag; the mock answers 404 for the unknown name).
#   CONTROL    on both Remotes the Cyrillic `pаypal` (а U+0430) is refused.
#   FIX #5840  `ascii_lookalikes: "Block"` is refused with 400.
#
# RED with main sha-0a3e08d as the candidate: no migration 421 (no stamp) and
# every ASCII lookalike refused, so the "new rule flags" case fails. GREEN
# with the artifact-keeper PR for #5840.
COVERS="5840"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-npm"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPGRADE_OLD_IMAGE="${UPGRADE_OLD_IMAGE:-ghcr.io/artifact-keeper/artifact-keeper-backend:1.12.4}"
